 "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Opening ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n15 February\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Closing ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n25 February\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition dates"] "\n" [:td {} "\n14 – 25 February\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Participants"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/counts/edition/34"} "693"] "\nfrom " [:a {:href "/editions/34/countries"} "30 countries"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Medal events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n22\nin 8 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Other events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n1\nin 1 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "\nOverview\n\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "The Olympic Winter Games were finally held in a Nordic country, and Norway worked hard to make these excellent Olympics.  The Norwegians renovated the famed " [:a {:href "/venues/99"} "Holmenkollen ski jump"] " for the 1952 Winter Olympics.  A restaurant and shops were placed under the take-off ramp, and an outdoor standing room stadium area was constructed, which allowed 130,000 people to watch the ski jumping events.  At the request of the " [:a {:href "/organizations/130"} "International Ice Hockey Federation"] ", the Norwegian Organizing Committee built an " [:a {:href "/venues/94"} "indoor ice hockey rink"] ", the first time that ice hockey was held indoors at the Olympics."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The 1952 Olympic Winter Games were opened on 15 February by " [:a {:href "/athletes/1100031"} "Her Royal Highness Princess Ragnhild"] ", granddaughter of the head of state.  She was chosen because her father, " [:a {:href "/athletes/63185"} "Crown Prince Olav"] ", and grandfather, King Haakon VII, were in London attending the funeral of " [:a {:href "/athletes/1100011"} "King George VI of Great Britain"] ".  For the first time, an Olympic flame was lit at the opening ceremony of the Olympic Winter Games.  Unlike the summer flame, however, this flame was originally lighted from the hearth of the house in Morgedal, Norway, where Sondre Norheim, the outstanding pioneer of modern skiing, was born.  At the end of a ski relay, the flame was lit in the " [:a {:href "/venues/93"} "Bislett Stadium"] " by " [:a {:href "/athletes/1100088"} "Eigil Nansen"] ", grandson of the explorer Fridtjof Nansen, whose book, " [:strong {} "The First Crossing of Greenland"] ", had given modern skiing its most important intellectual impetus."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The programme of events was changed only a little for 1952, with the addition of " [:a {:href "/results/1913"} "cross-country skiing for women"] ", and giant slalom Alpine skiing events for " [:a {:href "/results/1195"} "men"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/1200"} "women"] ".  The Alpine combined event was dropped from the programme, but it would eventually return, in " [:a {:href "/editions/43/sports/ALP"} "1988"] ".  Norway’s athletes dominated the events, especially so " [:a {:href "/athletes/83598"} "Hjalmar Andersen"] ", who won three gold medals in speed skating.  In Alpine skiing, the handsome " [:a {:href "/athletes/85544"} "Stein Eriksen"] " of Norway seemed the embodiment of a modern ski hero.  He won the giant slalom and was second in the " [:a {:href "/results/1196"} "slalom"] ".  " [:a {:href "/athletes/84513"} "Dick Button"] " of the United States won his second consecutive " [:a {:href "/results/12898"} "men’s figure skating"] " championship.  In women’s skiing, " [:a {:href "/athletes/81799"} "Andrea Mead-Lawrence"] " (USA) won two events in upsetting the European women.  One of these was the giant slalom, that was held on 14 February, for some reason, the day before the opening ceremony."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In bobsledding, Germany won both the " [:a {:href "/results/281"} "two-man"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/288"} "four-man"] " events.  Their “athletes” in these events were so large that their momentum helped them win by increasing their speed.  This caused the " [:a {:href "/organizations/128"} "International Bobsleigh Federation"] " to change its rules, to place a weight limit on bobsled teams."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Bid process"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "Bid voting at the 41st " [:a {:href "/meetings"} "IOC Session"] " in Stockholm, on 21 June 1947."] "\n\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped"} "\n  \t" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n  \t\t" [:th {}] "\n  \t\t" [:th {}] "\n  \t\t" [:th {} "Round 1"] "\n  \t"] [:tr {} "\n  \t\t" [:td {:class "city"} "Oslo"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "Norway"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "18"] "\n  \t"] [:tr {} "\n  \t\t" [:td {:class "city"} "Cortina d'Ampezzo"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "Italy"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "9"] "\n  \t"] [:tr {} "\n  \t\t" [:td {:class "city"} "Lake Placid, New York"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "United States"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "1"] "\n  "]]] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Ceremonies"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped table-condensed"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies/officially_opened"} "Officially opened by"]] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1100031"} "Ragnhild, Prinsesse av Norge"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {}] [:td {} "Her Royal Highness Princess"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies/torch_bearers"} "Torchbearer"]] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1700531"} "Olaf Mikkelsen Hemmestveit"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {}] [:td {} "First torch-bearer at Morgedal."] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {}] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1100088"} "Eigil Nansen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {}] [:td {} "Lit flam